# Anonymous Payment Architecture for a Privacy-Focused WireGuard VPN\n\n## Executive summary\n\nMullvad’s payment system is best understood as **data minimization and compartmentalization**, not universal payment anonymity. It creates random numbered accounts without names, passwords, or email addresses, and its WireGuard database associates the account number only with public keys and tunnel addresses. For card, PayPal, Swish, and bank payments, however, the financial intermediary can identify or substantially characterize the payer. Mullvad reduces exposure by giving processors an opaque payment token rather than the Mullvad account number and by deleting transaction identifiers after a short operational period. citeturn20view0turn20view2turn20view3turn21view3\n\nIts strongest payment methods are:\n\n| Method | Who can identify or correlate the payer? | Reversibility | Practical privacy |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| Mailed cash with an opaque token | Postal intermediaries or physical surveillance may observe the sender; Mullvad says it does not know who paid | No payment-rail chargeback | High, if sent without identifying information |\n| Self-hosted Monero | An exchange or wallet provider may know the buyer; blockchain analysis is reduced compared with transparent chains | No conventional chargeback | Potentially high |\n| Self-hosted Bitcoin or Bitcoin Cash | Exchange and blockchain observers may correlate the transaction | No conventional chargeback | Variable |\n| Reseller voucher bought with cash | Retailer sees a cash sale; VPN sees only redemption | Chargeback falls mainly on reseller | Very high provider-side separation |\n| Reseller voucher bought with card | Retailer and its processor know the buyer; VPN need not | Reseller bears card dispute exposure | High separation from VPN, not end-to-end anonymity |\n| Direct card or PayPal payment | Issuer, processor, wallet provider, and possibly merchant can access payment identity data | Chargebacks available | Pseudonymous to the VPN service at best |\n| Bank transfer | Banks normally know the sender and account | Lower reversal risk than cards, but not final in all circumstances | Poor anonymity |\n\nMullvad’s documented reseller design is especially important: scratch-card activation codes prevent the reseller from linking a retail payment to a Mullvad account. Yet Mullvad’s own no-logging document says its activation-code records contain the account number, voucher ID, and activation code. The system therefore protects against **third-party linkage**, but it is not necessarily internally unlinkable at the VPN operator. citeturn20view4turn20view6\n\nThe strongest architecture for a new service would improve on this by separating four domains:\n\n1. A payment domain that knows the processor transaction but never receives a VPN account or WireGuard key.\n2. A voucher or anonymous-entitlement issuer that converts settled value into a fixed-denomination bearer token.\n3. An entitlement domain that redeems the bearer token and adds time to a random account, without retaining a token-to-account mapping.\n4. A WireGuard control plane that stores only a derived service principal, public key, tunnel allocation, and entitlement status.\n\nFor ordinary implementations, high-entropy single-use vouchers are sufficient. For strong cryptographic unlinkability, use a reviewed blind-signature or oblivious-token protocol modeled on Privacy Pass: the payment system signs a blinded token, so it cannot recognize the token when it is later redeemed. Privacy Pass itself is an authorization framework rather than a payment product, but its IETF-standard issuance protocols demonstrate the necessary blind RSA and oblivious pseudorandom-function constructions. citeturn20view11\n\n**Chargebacks cannot be completely prevented while accepting ordinary credit or debit cards.** Successful 3-D Secure authentication usually shifts liability for stolen-card fraud to the issuer, but it does not eliminate disputes for non-fraud reasons, does not always shift liability, and can be lost if the merchant fails to answer an inquiry. citeturn20view8 The most privacy-preserving response is therefore not to build an identity-heavy anti-fraud system, but to use fixed low-value products, 3-D Secure, velocity controls, clear statement descriptors, simple refunds, reseller or merchant-of-record channels, and an explicitly budgeted chargeback-loss allowance.\n\nA payment system that accepts cards cannot honestly be called **fully anonymous**. The issuer ordinarily knows the cardholder, the payment service provider sees the transaction, and the merchant must undergo business and beneficial-owner verification. A VPN can nevertheless achieve strong **merchant-side unlinkability**: the processor sees a random purchase handle, the VPN never receives card details, and the entitlement is redeemed through an unlinkable voucher. Stripe Elements, for example, tokenizes card information without it touching the merchant’s server, while Stripe’s onboarding requirements can require business, representative, and beneficial-owner information from the merchant. citeturn21view8turn21view9\n\n## Mullvad’s documented payment model\n\n### Account and WireGuard identity\n\nMullvad’s starting point is an anonymous account capability rather than a conventional customer profile. Account creation produces a random numbered account without an email address, name, username, or password. Its documented account record contains:\n\n```text\naccount_number | expiry_date\n```\n\nIts documented WireGuard configuration record contains:\n\n```text\naccount_number | public_key | tunnel_address\n```\n\nThis is a meaningful privacy improvement over an email-based identity system: payment and service access are associated with an arbitrary capability rather than an obvious real-world identifier. It does not, by itself, make payments anonymous or prevent correlations through timestamps, processor records, network metadata, support interactions, or the operator’s internal databases. citeturn20view0\n\nThe account number is effectively both an identifier and a bearer credential. Mullvad’s terms state that it is the only confirmation of the right to use the account and request a refund. This simplifies account recovery and minimizes personal data, but account-number compromise can enable account takeover; a comparable implementation should therefore support locally generated recovery or delegation secrets without requiring an email address. citeturn20view5\n\n### Mailed cash\n\nMullvad instructs the payer to place currency and a **payment token**, not the account number, in an envelope. Mullvad opens the envelope, uses the token to credit the account, and shreds the non-currency contents. It asks senders not to include names or return addresses. Its documented persistent record contains the payment record, account number, amount, currency, and timestamp. citeturn21view0\n\nThe conceptual flow is:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant U as User\n    participant W as Mullvad website\n    participant P as Postal system\n    participant C as Cash-processing function\n    participant A as Account ledger\n\n    U->>W: Request cash-payment instructions\n    W-->>U: Opaque payment token\n    U->>P: Mail cash plus payment token\n    P->>C: Deliver envelope\n    C->>C: Read token and count currency\n    C->>A: Add time to token-associated account\n    C->>C: Shred non-currency contents\n```\n\nThis protects the account number from postal handlers and prevents a written account number from becoming part of the mailed artifact. It does **not** make the entire process immune to postal tracking, fingerprints, handwriting, cameras, registered-mail records, source-address leakage, or timing analysis. Mullvad itself recommends avoiding identifying information. citeturn21view0\n\nOperationally, cash is expensive: it requires secure physical custody, dual-control counting, counterfeit detection, exchange-rate rules, theft reconciliation, and safe deposit procedures. IVPN illustrates the scalability problem: it accepts cash only for one-, two-, or three-year purchases, says processing can take one to four weeks, and makes those payments non-refundable. citeturn21view6\n\n### Cryptocurrency\n\nMullvad accepts Bitcoin, Bitcoin Cash, and Monero. It states that it operates its own full nodes and self-hosts its wallets, avoiding an external payment processor. For cryptocurrency records, it retains amount, currency, and timestamp; after 20 days it truncates the timestamp to the date. Unique Bitcoin and Bitcoin Cash receiving addresses are also removed after 20 days. Mullvad retains the Monero transaction hash longer to prevent the same transaction from being credited twice. citeturn21view1\n\nThis is materially more private than using a hosted cryptocurrency processor because a hosted processor can learn invoice values, addresses, timestamps, IP metadata, merchant identity, and settlement details. BTCPay Server provides the corresponding implementation model for Bitcoin: the merchant runs the software and node, validates payments itself, and avoids a custodial intermediary, at the cost of greater operational complexity. citeturn20view9\n\nCryptocurrency is not synonymous with anonymity:\n\n- A regulated exchange may know the payer and withdrawal address.\n- Bitcoin and Bitcoin Cash have publicly visible transaction graphs.\n- Invoice amount and timing can correlate a blockchain transaction with account activation.\n- A self-hosted wallet removes a payment processor but does not eliminate blockchain, endpoint, exchange, or network metadata.\n- Monero offers a stronger default privacy model, but exchange records, compromised endpoints, timing, malware, and operational mistakes remain relevant.\n\nFor maximum separation, a cryptocurrency payment should buy an **unlinked bearer entitlement**, rather than directly extend an identified service account. The user would pay an invoice, receive a token, and redeem it in a separate session, ideally after a delay and through a different network path.\n\n### Cards, PayPal, Swish, and bank transfers\n\nMullvad uses Stripe for cards, PayPal for PayPal payments, and SEB for Swish and bank transfers. Its policy explicitly says these methods are not anonymous because the third parties maintain records showing who paid Mullvad. citeturn20view2turn21view1\n\nMullvad says it can access the following categories through its payment providers:\n\n| Rail | Potentially accessible payment data |\n|---|---|\n| Bank wire | Sender name, address, and bank account number |\n| PayPal | Transaction ID, sender name, origin country, and email |\n| Swish | Swish ID, name, and phone number |\n| Stripe | Charge ID, card expiry, last four digits, card type, and origin country |\n\nciteturn21view2turn21view3\n\nThe key privacy control is the intermediary token. Mullvad says PayPal and Stripe do not receive the Mullvad account number; it sends them a token that it uses internally to link the successful payment to the account. Bank transfers are a weaker exception because the account number appears in the transfer’s message field. Mullvad keeps transaction identifiers to handle its refund period and payment errors and deletes the transaction ID after 20 days, while accounting information may remain for statutory periods. citeturn20view3turn21view4\n\nThis is a sound minimization pattern, but the exact protection should be described accurately:\n\n```text\nProcessor:\npayer identity/payment instrument <-> processor transaction <-> opaque Mullvad token\n\nMullvad:\nopaque Mullvad token <-> account number <-> entitlement\n```\n\nThe processor cannot directly identify the Mullvad account from the token alone. Mullvad, however, can perform the internal token-to-account join while the mapping exists. A subpoena, compromise, insider, or coordinated request during the retention window could potentially combine the two datasets.\n\nA stronger design would make the processor token authorize issuance of a **separate bearer voucher** and would delete—or cryptographically prevent—the payment-to-voucher relationship.\n\n### Reseller vouchers and partner channels\n\nMullvad’s official scratch cards contain an activation code that can add time to a new or existing account. Mullvad says the design prevents third parties from linking the retail payment to a Mullvad account and allows a card to be given to another person without the purchaser knowing the recipient’s account number. citeturn20view6\n\nIts no-logging policy nevertheless documents an activation-code table containing:\n\n```text\npayment | account_number | amount | timestamp | voucher_id | activation_code\n```\n\nIt separately says that accounts created by partners have no stored payment information; the provider stores only events recording when time was added or removed. citeturn20view4\n\nThe likely privacy boundary is therefore:\n\n- The reseller knows the purchase and possibly the voucher serial.\n- The reseller does not receive the eventual Mullvad account number.\n- Mullvad may retain the voucher-to-account relationship internally.\n- Partner-created accounts can be cleaner because Mullvad receives an entitlement adjustment rather than retail payment data.\n\nFor a new implementation, the activation-code record should **not** permanently retain `account_id`. After an atomic redemption, the voucher database should retain only that the token has been spent, while the account ledger records only that an anonymous entitlement was added.\n\nMullvad does not refund cash, cryptocurrency, or vouchers. Card, PayPal, Swish, bank-wire, and some app-store payments can be covered by its 14-day policy; reseller-voucher disputes go to the reseller because Mullvad lacks the retail payment data. citeturn20view7 This arrangement is not merely a refund policy—it is also a risk-separation mechanism.\n\n## Provider comparison and payment-stack choices\n\nThe following comparison focuses on publicly documented payment architecture, not overall VPN quality or anonymity guarantees.\n\n| VPN provider | Account model and anonymous methods | Separation characteristics | Chargeback exposure | Implementation complexity | Principal advantages and limitations |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Mullvad** | Random numbered account; mailed cash; self-hosted Bitcoin, Bitcoin Cash, and Monero; scratch-card resellers; cards via Stripe; PayPal; Swish; bank wire | Processor receives an opaque token rather than account number; reseller cannot link purchase to account; internal voucher/account and payment/account records can still exist | High for direct cards and PayPal; low at VPN level for reseller vouchers; no conventional chargeback for cash or direct crypto | High | Best-documented combination of anonymous accounts, cash, self-hosted crypto, and retail vouchers. Bank transfer explicitly exposes the account number in the message field. citeturn20view0turn20view3turn20view6turn21view1 |\n| **IVPN** | Generated account ID without required email or name; cash; self-hosted Monero; Bitcoin through its own BTCPay Server; physical and digital vouchers; cards through Braintree; PayPal | IVPN says no third-party payment provider receives the IVPN account ID. Voucher code is linked to the account for 30 days and then deleted | Card and PayPal disputes remain; cash and vouchers are non-refundable; crypto has no card-style dispute rail | High | Closest documented comparator to Mullvad. Its 30-day voucher-link deletion is a useful explicit retention control, though temporary internal linkage remains. citeturn19view4turn21view5turn21view7 |\n| **Proton VPN** | Cards, PayPal, Apple Pay, Google Pay, Bitcoin, cash, and bank transfers | Cash must contain the Proton username; bank transfer requires the username plus proof containing the payer’s name, date, and amount; Bitcoin becomes available after account creation | High for card and wallet rails; bank and cash payments are documented as non-refundable, subject to overriding law | Medium | Broad payment support, but cash and bank flows are explicitly account-linked and therefore weaker for payer anonymity than Mullvad or IVPN. citeturn19view5 |\n| **Private Internet Access** | Cards, PayPal, Amazon Pay, app stores; non-recurring crypto through BitPay; Paymentwall wallets; gift cards; prepaid Visa and Mastercard | Anonymous-account or internal unlinkability guarantees are not documented on the cited payment page; third parties mediate many methods | High for cards, wallets, and prepaid cards; reseller/gift-card allocation depends on commercial arrangements | Low to medium | Broad consumer convenience and lower implementation burden, but more processor dependence and weaker documented separation. Its prepaid cards require issuer registration and matching billing details. citeturn19view6 |\n\n### Recommended payment providers by role\n\nThere is no universally “best” provider; the correct choice depends on jurisdiction, underwriting willingness, transaction volume, corporate structure, and threat model.\n\n| Requirement | Preferred approach | Why | Main trade-off |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Direct Bitcoin with minimal intermediary data | Self-hosted BTCPay Server and a merchant-controlled node | Non-custodial, open-source, direct validation, no hosted processor required | Node security, backups, liquidity, monitoring, and invoice reconciliation become the operator’s responsibility. citeturn20view9 |\n| Direct Monero | Self-hosted node and wallet infrastructure | Avoids a hosted crypto processor and provides stronger on-chain privacy than transparent ledgers | Operational expertise, exchange availability, accounting valuation, sanctions controls, and wallet security |\n| Low-friction cards for a smaller operator | Stripe-hosted Checkout or Stripe Elements, with no Stripe Customer object unless necessary | Sensitive card details can be tokenized without reaching the VPN server; mature 3-D Secure and dispute tooling | Stripe still knows the transaction and must verify the merchant; processor policy and account-closure risk remain. citeturn20view8turn21view8turn21view9 |\n| Card alternative with a documented privacy-VPN precedent | Braintree | IVPN uses Braintree while withholding the IVPN account ID from it | Similar card-network, KYC, dispute, and processor-retention limitations to other acquirers. citeturn19view4 |\n| High-volume international acquiring | Adyen, subject to commercial acceptance | Sophisticated risk and 3-D Secure capabilities and direct enterprise acquiring in many markets | More demanding onboarding and integration; business and user verification obligations vary by product and structure. citeturn14search2turn14search6 |\n| Maximum payment/account separation | Independent voucher reseller or merchant of record | Retailer handles payer-facing card data and disputes; VPN receives only prepaid inventory settlement or entitlement events | Fees, loss of checkout control, reseller KYC, reseller data retention, contractual dependency, and possible voucher-to-sale correlation |\n| Cash distribution at scale | Physical scratch cards sold through resellers | Avoids the VPN processing envelopes and lets customers pay cash locally | Inventory theft, counterfeiting, retail margins, activation design, and geographic availability |\n\nFor a privacy service, the preferred stack is therefore not one processor. It is a **portfolio**:\n\n- Cards through a hosted PCI-compliant checkout, used only to sell fixed-denomination anonymous vouchers.\n- Direct self-hosted Bitcoin and Monero.\n- Physical and digital vouchers through independent resellers.\n- Mailed cash only if staffing, insurance, physical security, and volume justify it.\n- Bank transfer as a convenience method clearly labeled non-anonymous.\n- No mandatory recurring subscription for privacy-sensitive customers.\n\n## Technical designs for payment, entitlement, and WireGuard separation\n\n### Threat model and privacy definitions\n\nBefore implementation, the service should define which parties must be unable to correlate which events.\n\n| Privacy property | Desired result |\n|---|---|\n| Processor separation | Processor cannot see VPN account number, WireGuard key, tunnel address, or selected VPN server |\n| VPN payment-team separation | Payment staff cannot retrieve WireGuard keys or service activity |\n| Control-plane separation | WireGuard systems cannot retrieve processor transaction IDs, card attributes, bank details, or voucher retail records |\n| Reseller separation | Reseller cannot determine the account on which a voucher was redeemed |\n| Internal unlinkability | Even a database administrator cannot perform a direct payment-to-account join |\n| Collusion resistance | Processor and VPN cannot correlate users through a shared identifier |\n| Timing resistance | Payment and redemption cannot easily be joined by exact amount and timestamp |\n| Global-observer anonymity | A network-level observer cannot correlate payment, redemption, application use, and VPN traffic |\n\nA Mullvad-style opaque payment token delivers mainly **processor separation**. Conventional vouchers add **reseller separation**. Blindly issued bearer entitlements can provide strong **internal unlinkability and collusion resistance**, but exact timing, denomination, browser metadata, IP addresses, and support interactions can still defeat the design. No payment architecture alone provides global-observer anonymity.\n\n### Baseline compartmentalized architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph PaymentZone["Payment and accounting zone"]\n        PSP["Card / bank / PayPal / crypto interface"]\n        PL["Payment ledger"]\n        RI["Refund interface"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph IssuanceZone["Entitlement issuance zone"]\n        VI["Voucher issuer"]\n        KMS["KMS / HSM signing keys"]\n        BL["Batch and liability ledger"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph RedemptionZone["Account and redemption zone"]\n        VR["Voucher redemption"]\n        AC["Random account ledger"]\n        EL["Entitlement events"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph VPNZone["WireGuard control plane"]\n        AU["Capability authentication"]\n        WG["WireGuard peer database"]\n        GW["VPN gateways"]\n    end\n\n    PSP --> PL\n    PL -->|"settled purchase authorization; no account ID"| VI\n    KMS --> VI\n    VI -->|"bearer code or signed token"| VR\n    VR -->|"add duration; discard token/account join"| EL\n    EL --> AC\n    AC --> AU\n    AU -->|"derived service principal"| WG\n    WG --> GW\n    RI --> PL\n```\n\nThe critical rule is that **no identifier appears in all zones**. In particular:\n\n- `processor_transaction_id` never enters the redemption or VPN zones.\n- `account_id` never enters the payment zone.\n- Voucher plaintext never enters permanent logs.\n- WireGuard public keys never enter payment or accounting systems.\n- IP addresses from VPN traffic never enter payment-fraud systems.\n- Support tooling cannot automatically query both payment and VPN data.\n\n### Suggested database schemas\n\n#### Payment ledger\n\n```sql\npayments (\n    payment_id              UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    processor               ENUM,\n    processor_reference_enc BYTEA,\n    amount_minor            BIGINT,\n    currency                CHAR(3),\n    product_class           SMALLINT,\n    status                  ENUM,\n    created_day             DATE,\n    exact_created_at_enc    BYTEA NULL,\n    refund_secret_mac       BYTEA NULL,\n    issuance_authorization  BYTEA,\n    retention_class         SMALLINT\n)\n```\n\nImportant properties:\n\n- No account number, voucher plaintext, public key, email, VPN IP, or device identifier.\n- Encrypt processor references under a payment-zone key.\n- Reduce exact timestamps to date buckets after the refund and reconciliation period.\n- Avoid creating reusable processor “customer” profiles for one-time purchases unless required.\n- Keep recurring billing opt-in and separate from anonymous prepaid products.\n\n#### Voucher inventory\n\n```sql\nvoucher_batches (\n    batch_id          UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    product_class     SMALLINT,\n    duration_seconds  BIGINT,\n    quantity          INTEGER,\n    distributor_id    UUID NULL,\n    created_day       DATE,\n    signing_key_id    UUID,\n    status            ENUM\n);\n\nvouchers (\n    code_mac          BYTEA PRIMARY KEY,\n    batch_id          UUID REFERENCES voucher_batches,\n    state             ENUM,\n    issued_day        DATE,\n    redeemed_day      DATE NULL,\n    revocation_epoch  INTEGER,\n    redemption_nonce  BYTEA NULL\n);\n```\n\nThe voucher table deliberately has **no account ID**. The raw code is never stored after production. Store:\n\n```text\ncode_mac = HMAC-SHA-256(voucher_pepper, canonical_code)\n```\n\nThe pepper belongs in a KMS or HSM and should not be available to the ordinary database role.\n\n#### Accounts and entitlements\n\n```sql\naccounts (\n    account_id          BYTEA PRIMARY KEY,\n    expires_at          TIMESTAMP,\n    account_state       ENUM,\n    recovery_commitment BYTEA NULL,\n    created_day         DATE\n);\n\nentitlement_events (\n    event_id          UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    account_id        BYTEA,\n    duration_seconds  BIGINT,\n    source_class      ENUM,      -- voucher, promotional, support adjustment\n    product_class     SMALLINT,\n    created_day       DATE,\n    audit_commitment  BYTEA\n);\n```\n\n`source_class` should not contain a payment ID, voucher ID, reseller order number, or exact payment timestamp.\n\n#### WireGuard peer records\n\n```sql\nwireguard_peers (\n    peer_id             UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    service_principal   BYTEA,\n    public_key          BYTEA UNIQUE,\n    tunnel_address      INET,\n    state               ENUM,\n    created_day         DATE,\n    rotation_generation INTEGER,\n    replacement_peer_id UUID NULL\n);\n```\n\nRather than storing the raw account capability, derive:\n\n```text\nservice_principal = HMAC(K_service_principal, account_id)\n```\n\nThe authentication service checks entitlement and returns only the derived principal and policy. The WireGuard database cannot reverse it without the authentication-domain key.\n\nWireGuard private keys should be generated on the client. WireGuard’s official quick-start procedure generates a private key locally and derives its public key; only the public key needs to be registered with the server. citeturn20view10\n\n### Voucher generation and redemption\n\nVoucher codes should contain at least 128 bits of cryptographically secure randomness; 160 bits leaves more margin for transcription-friendly encodings and future scale. A practical format is Crockford Base32 divided into groups, plus an error-detection checksum:\n\n```text\n7K4M2-9ZJQF-W6TD8-H3XNP-4RV\n```\n\nRecommended generation logic:\n\n```text\nrandom_payload = CSPRNG(160 bits)\nproduct_id     = authenticated metadata, not necessarily visible\nchecksum       = checksum(version || random_payload)\ndisplay_code   = base32(version || random_payload || checksum)\ndatabase_key   = HMAC(voucher_pepper, canonical(display_code))\n```\n\nDo not encode sequential batch numbers, dates, reseller IDs, or monetary values directly in the visible code. Those fields facilitate enumeration and correlation.\n\nThe redemption transaction must be atomic:\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n\nSELECT state, batch_id\nFROM vouchers\nWHERE code_mac = :code_mac\nFOR UPDATE;\n\n-- Reject invalid, redeemed, expired, or revoked tokens.\n\nUPDATE vouchers\nSET state = 'redeemed',\n    redeemed_day = CURRENT_DATE,\n    redemption_nonce = :idempotency_nonce\nWHERE code_mac = :code_mac\n  AND state = 'active';\n\nINSERT INTO entitlement_events (...);\n\nUPDATE accounts\nSET expires_at = GREATEST(expires_at, NOW()) + :duration\nWHERE account_id = :account_id;\n\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nAfter the transaction, no table should retain both `code_mac` and `account_id`. This means support cannot recover a lost account from a voucher after redemption. That is a deliberate privacy trade-off; IVPN, by contrast, retains the code-to-account association for 30 days to permit recovery and then deletes it. citeturn21view7\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant U as User client\n    participant R as Redemption API\n    participant V as Voucher database\n    participant E as Entitlement ledger\n    participant A as Account database\n    participant W as WireGuard control plane\n\n    U->>R: account capability + voucher code + idempotency nonce\n    R->>R: Normalize code; compute keyed MAC\n    R->>V: Lock voucher row\n    V-->>R: Active voucher and product class\n    R->>V: Mark spent; store no account ID\n    R->>E: Append anonymous-source entitlement event\n    R->>A: Extend account expiry\n    R-->>U: Redemption successful\n    U->>U: Generate WireGuard private/public key\n    U->>W: Account proof + public key\n    W->>A: Check active entitlement through auth service\n    A-->>W: Derived service principal and policy\n    W-->>U: Tunnel address and server configuration\n```\n\n### Blindly issued entitlements\n\nA conventional voucher issuer can log which exact code was created for a particular card payment. To prevent this, the user can generate and blind the token before payment authorization.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant U as User browser or app\n    participant P as Card processor\n    participant I as Blind-token issuer\n    participant R as Redemption service\n    participant A as Account ledger\n\n    U->>U: Generate token nonce and blind it\n    U->>P: Pay fixed denomination with random purchase handle\n    P-->>I: Signed webhook: purchase settled\n    U->>I: Purchase authorization + blinded token request\n    I->>I: Verify authorization and mark issuance allowance consumed\n    I-->>U: Blind signature\n    U->>U: Unblind signature\n    Note over I,U: Issuer never sees the final token\n    U->>R: Redeem signed bearer token in a later session\n    R->>R: Verify signature and one-time-spend status\n    R->>A: Add entitlement to random account\n    R-->>U: Success\n```\n\nThe IETF’s Privacy Pass issuance standards show two relevant families:\n\n- Privately verifiable tokens based on an oblivious pseudorandom function.\n- Publicly verifiable tokens based on blind RSA signatures.\n\nThey allow a client to prove only that a valid issuer created a token in the past, rather than disclose the issuance transaction. citeturn20view11\n\nA production payment adaptation requires independent cryptographic review. Particular hazards include:\n\n- Issuing unique denominations that identify individual purchases.\n- Embedding timestamps, payment IDs, or customer IDs in signed metadata.\n- Reusing blinding factors or weak randomness.\n- Creating an issuance endpoint whose cookies or IP logs identify redemption.\n- Signing different public keys for different processors or users.\n- Failure to implement double-spend detection.\n- Side channels through response sizes, errors, timing, or signature-key selection.\n\nUse a small number of fixed denominations—such as 30, 90, and 365 days—and issue tokens in batches. Redemption should be allowed long after payment, and the payment and redemption domains should have different cookies, hostnames, logging systems, encryption keys, and administrative teams.\n\n### WireGuard key rotation\n\nKey rotation must be independent of payment events. Rotating a key immediately after renewal creates a timestamp correlation between payment, entitlement, and network records.\n\nA safer sequence is:\n\n1. The client generates the replacement private key locally.\n2. The client sends the new public key while authenticated with the account capability.\n3. The control plane installs the replacement as a second peer.\n4. Both keys are accepted for a short overlap.\n5. The client confirms connectivity using the new key.\n6. The old key and historical mapping are deleted after the grace interval.\n7. Rotation audit records retain only generation counters and coarse dates unless more is legally or operationally necessary.\n\nFor a stronger design, the WireGuard service can use an epoch-specific principal:\n\n```text\nepoch_principal = HMAC(K_epoch, account_id || epoch_number)\n```\n\nThis limits cross-epoch correlation in the gateway database. The authentication layer still needs to recognize the account, but gateway operators cannot trivially join peer records across epochs.\n\n## Chargebacks, fraud controls, and ethical boundaries\n\n### Chargebacks by rail\n\n| Payment method | Can the merchant completely prevent reversal? | Typical provider risk | Privacy-compatible mitigation |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Credit or debit card | No | High | 3-D Secure, clear descriptors, low fixed values, velocity limits, easy refunds, reseller or merchant-of-record distribution |\n| PayPal and card-backed wallets | No | High | Similar controls to cards; assume wallet and underlying card dispute mechanisms remain |\n| Apple Pay or Google Pay | No | Medium to high | Tokenization and device authentication can reduce fraud, but disputes still follow the card rail |\n| SEPA Direct Debit | No | High | Avoid for anonymous prepaid access; EU law provides an unconditional refund period for qualifying direct debits |\n| Bank credit transfer | Not absolutely | Low to medium | Wait for settled funds; handle fraud recalls and mistaken transfers; never describe the rail as anonymous |\n| Bitcoin, Bitcoin Cash, or Monero paid directly | No card-network chargeback | Low after confirmation | Confirmation thresholds, invoice expiry, underpayment handling, secure wallets, clear refund policy |\n| Mailed cash | No electronic chargeback | Physical-loss risk | Dual custody, video-controlled counting where lawful, reconciliation, counterfeit controls |\n| Voucher sold by an independent reseller | VPN generally avoids retail card dispute | Low for VPN; high for reseller | Wholesale settlement, batch reconciliation, fraud reserve, clear allocation of refund and stolen-inventory liability |\n\nIn the EU, PSD2 provides an unconditional refund right for qualifying direct debits within an eight-week period. A “no refund” clause cannot contract away mandatory rights where they apply. citeturn17view1 In the United States, credit-card users also have statutory billing-error and dispute procedures; the CFPB describes a 60-day notice period for specified billing errors. citeturn15search3turn15search7\n\n### What 3-D Secure does and does not do\n\nSuccessful 3-D Secure authentication typically shifts liability for fraudulent stolen- or counterfeit-card claims to the issuer. It does not guarantee protection where:\n\n- Authentication fails or is unavailable.\n- The network or business category excludes liability shift.\n- The merchant has excessive fraud or is in a monitoring program.\n- Liability is downgraded after authorization.\n- The dispute alleges service not received, cancellation, misrepresentation, or another non-fraud reason.\n- The merchant ignores a request for information, resulting in a no-reply chargeback.\n\nStripe documents all of these limitations. citeturn20view8\n\nTherefore, “require 3DS and eliminate chargebacks” is not a viable design. A realistic model is:\n\n```text\nexpected card loss =\nfraud disputes not shifted\n+ non-fraud disputes\n+ processor fees\n+ dispute fees\n+ operational handling\n+ uncollectible negative balances\n```\n\nFor a low-cost VPN product, this expected loss may be cheaper and less privacy-invasive than extensive device fingerprinting or customer identity verification.\n\n### Privacy-preserving fraud controls\n\nRecommended controls include:\n\n- Fixed-denomination products and maximum purchase limits.\n- 3-D Secure when available or risk-triggered.\n- Webhook signature validation and idempotency.\n- Rate limits on purchase sessions, voucher issuance, and failed redemption.\n- One-time issuance authorizations and atomic voucher spending.\n- Short-lived fraud signals rather than indefinite device profiles.\n- Coarse country or network-risk classifications, deleted after the dispute period.\n- No storage of complete IP addresses unless demonstrably necessary and lawful.\n- No merging of VPN connection data with payment-fraud data.\n- A recognizable statement descriptor and immediate purchase receipt.\n- A self-service refund process requiring a payment-specific secret, not a VPN account number.\n- Clear notice that the product is prepaid and when service begins.\n- Evidence that an entitlement was issued and redeemed, without recording VPN destinations, traffic, DNS queries, or server sessions.\n\nA card purchase can return two independent secrets:\n\n```text\nentitlement_token   -> redeemable for VPN time\nrefund_secret       -> usable only to identify the processor transaction for refund\n```\n\nThe payment database stores only `HMAC(refund_secret)` with the processor transaction. The user voluntarily reveals the refund secret when seeking a refund. Routine redemption never creates a payment-to-account link.\n\n### Fundamental chargeback versus unlinkability trade-off\n\nPerfect unlinkability and post-redemption revocation conflict.\n\nSuppose a cardholder pays, receives an unlinkable voucher, redeems it, and later disputes the card charge. If the operator truly cannot link the payment to the redeemed account, it cannot revoke that account. The available choices are:\n\n| Design | Privacy | Chargeback recovery |\n|---|---|---|\n| Retain payment-to-account mapping | Weak | Account can be suspended |\n| Retain payment-to-voucher mapping | Moderate | Redeemed voucher may identify account if redemption mapping also exists |\n| Delete voucher-to-account mapping but retain payment-to-voucher | Stronger | Future voucher can be blocked, but already-added time cannot be located |\n| Blindly issued entitlement | Strong | No technical revocation of the redeemed account |\n| Reseller or merchant-of-record voucher | Strong at VPN | Reseller absorbs or manages the retail dispute |\n| Delay entitlement until dispute window expires | Poor customer experience | Reduces some risk but does not cover all later disputes |\n\nThe recommended answer is usually to **accept bounded losses rather than destroy the privacy property**. Use low-value vouchers, 3-D Secure, a fraud reserve, and reseller risk transfer. Attempting to make an anonymous card voucher fully recoverable after chargeback normally reintroduces a correlation handle.\n\n### Legally and ethically dangerous practices\n\nThe following strategies should not be used:\n\n- Misrepresenting the product category to the acquirer.\n- Using an unrelated or misleading merchant descriptor.\n- Routing payments through a shell merchant to hide that the product is a VPN.\n- Falsifying delivery evidence or customer consent.\n- Claiming that a chargeback waiver overrides mandatory consumer law.\n- Threatening to publish customer identities or VPN activity after a dispute.\n- Combining traffic logs with payment data solely to build stronger dispute evidence.\n- Buying identity or device data from brokers without a documented lawful basis and clear notice.\n- Automatically treating every dispute as fraud.\n- Operating a multi-merchant transferable voucher as an unlicensed payment or money-transmission product.\n\nThese measures create contractual, consumer-protection, privacy, fraud, licensing, and reputational risks. The ethical design goal is to reduce accidental disputes and stolen-card use while preserving the service’s promised privacy boundaries.\n\n## Cards, banks, KYC, and legal constraints\n\n### Can a fully anonymous system accept cards?\n\nNot in the ordinary meaning of “fully anonymous.”\n\nA normal card transaction involves several distinct knowledge domains:\n\n```text\nCard issuer:\ncardholder identity, account, authentication, transaction\n\nCard network:\ntransaction routing metadata\n\nAcquirer / PSP:\nmerchant identity, amount, time, payment instrument attributes,\nauthentication and fraud data\n\nVPN merchant:\nwhatever checkout, webhook, processor, and internal linkage data it chooses to keep\n```\n\nThe VPN can avoid seeing the card number by using hosted fields or a hosted checkout. Stripe Elements tokenizes payment details inside the payment component without passing the sensitive card data through the merchant’s server. citeturn21view9 That reduces PCI scope and breach impact, but Stripe and the issuer still process the payment.\n\nThe merchant itself cannot ordinarily be anonymous to the acquirer. Stripe’s documentation says verification may require company details, the representative’s name and date of birth, beneficial-owner information, government identification, and proof of address, depending on country and risk. citeturn21view8 Adyen similarly describes KYC verification as a prerequisite for payment and payout capabilities in applicable platform arrangements. citeturn14search2turn14search6\n\nThe defensible claim is therefore:\n\n> Card customers can be pseudonymous to the VPN account system, and their card payment can be technically separated from their WireGuard identity. The card payment itself is not anonymous to the financial institutions processing it.\n\n### Three workable card architectures\n\n#### Direct card purchase with an opaque handle\n\nThe processor receives a random `purchase_handle`; the VPN stores a temporary mapping from that handle to a payment and then issues time to an account.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- Simple.\n- Refund and revocation support.\n- Similar to Mullvad’s documented processor-token flow.\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n- The VPN can perform the join.\n- Payment and account become correlatable during retention.\n- A compromise or legal demand can combine records.\n\n#### Card-purchased voucher\n\nThe card purchase returns a bearer voucher. Redemption occurs separately, and the voucher-to-account mapping is immediately deleted.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- Processor sees no account identifier.\n- VPN’s long-term account database contains no payment reference.\n- Straightforward to implement.\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n- The voucher issuer can still know which code corresponded to which card transaction unless issuance records are deleted.\n- Exact payment and redemption times can correlate.\n- A redeemed account cannot be found after a chargeback.\n\n#### Blind card-purchased voucher\n\nThe card payment authorizes a blind signature over a user-created token.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- The issuer cannot recognize the final voucher.\n- Strongest cryptographic separation.\n- Processor and redemption database have no common identifier.\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n- Complex protocol and key management.\n- Difficult refunds, fraud revocation, and customer support.\n- Timing and browser metadata can still correlate events.\n- Requires independent cryptographic review and careful denomination design.\n\n### Bank-transfer feasibility\n\nBank transfers are operationally simple but normally poor for anonymity. Banks process account-holder and transfer information, and the beneficiary may receive names, account identifiers, addresses, references, and country information depending on the rail. Mullvad acknowledges access to sender name, address, and bank account number for bank wires; Proton requires a username in the transfer description and proof containing the payer’s full name, date, and amount. citeturn21view2turn19view5\n\nA bank transfer can still be decoupled from the VPN account:\n\n1. The customer requests a random bank-payment reference.\n2. The bank reconciliation system sees the reference and payment.\n3. Once settled, it issues a bearer voucher.\n4. The customer redeems the voucher separately.\n5. The bank-payment reference is never stored with the VPN account.\n\nThat creates provider-side separation, but the bank still knows who transferred money, and exact amounts or timestamps can enable correlation.\n\nDirect debit is generally unsuitable for anonymous prepaid service. It requires an ongoing mandate and, in the EU, carries strong refund rights. citeturn17view1\n\n### Voucher and stored-value regulation\n\nA single-merchant, non-transferable, non-cash-redeemable voucher for a narrowly defined service is less likely to resemble general-purpose money than a multi-merchant stored-value wallet. It may nevertheless be regulated depending on the jurisdiction, distribution model, transferability, expiration, refundability, and whether the operator holds customer funds.\n\nUnder EU PSD2, services based on instruments that can be used only in the issuer’s premises, within a limited network, or for a very limited range of goods or services may fall within the limited-network exclusion. citeturn16view3 The European Banking Authority identifies store cards, fuel cards, public-transport cards, and meal vouchers as examples that may qualify, while emphasizing the limited character of the network. citeturn18search3turn18search7\n\nA VPN voucher is more defensible as a closed-loop product when it:\n\n- Can buy only that VPN’s fixed-duration service.\n- Is not redeemable for cash or cryptocurrency.\n- Does not allow person-to-person balances.\n- Cannot be loaded and reloaded as a general wallet.\n- Is not accepted by unrelated merchants.\n- Does not pay reseller commissions through transferable customer balances.\n- Has clear denomination, expiry, loss, refund, and jurisdictional terms.\n\nExpanding it into a multi-provider “privacy services card” may trigger payment-services, e-money, money-transmission, AML, safeguarding, reporting, or licensing obligations.\n\nIn the United States, FinCEN distinguishes users of convertible virtual currency from administrators and exchangers; a person acquiring virtual currency to buy goods or services is treated differently from a business that accepts and transmits or exchanges value for others. Whether a merchant, voucher issuer, or crypto intermediary becomes a money transmitter is fact-specific. citeturn18search2turn18search34\n\n### Privacy law and retention\n\nEuropean data-protection principles support the proposed separation. The GDPR defines pseudonymization as keeping the additional identifying information separately under technical and organizational controls. It requires purpose limitation, data minimization, storage limitation, integrity, confidentiality, and accountability. citeturn16view0turn16view2 Article 25 further requires data protection by design and default, including measures such as pseudonymization and limiting accessibility and retention to what is necessary. citeturn17view0\n\nArticle 11 is particularly relevant: where the processing purpose does not require identification, the controller is not obligated to acquire or retain additional information solely to identify the person. citeturn16view1 This supports not collecting an email address or legal name merely to administer a VPN account.\n\nIt does not mean all pseudonymous identifiers fall outside privacy law. Account numbers, voucher records, payment tokens, public keys, IP addresses, and timestamps may still be personal data where a person can reasonably be singled out or records can be combined.\n\nMullvad illustrates the retention conflict. It deletes payment transaction IDs after 20 days but says some accounting data may need to remain for seven years under Swedish accounting law. citeturn21view4 A new operator should therefore use a dedicated accounting enclave containing only legally required fields, with no account, voucher, WireGuard, or network identifier.\n\n### Consumer law\n\nRefund and withdrawal rules vary significantly. In the EU, distance contracts generally have a 14-day withdrawal right, subject to exceptions and conditions. For digital content, loss of the withdrawal right requires prior express consent to begin performance, acknowledgement of the loss of the right, and confirmation from the trader. Service contracts have related but not identical rules. citeturn17view3turn15search6\n\nA VPN operator should not assume that labeling a voucher “non-refundable” resolves these issues. The legal classification may differ among:\n\n- An unused bearer voucher.\n- A redeemed voucher where service has not started.\n- A prepaid service already partially performed.\n- A recurring subscription.\n- Digital content supplied immediately.\n- A business-to-business purchase.\n- A reseller sale where the reseller is the consumer-facing merchant.\n\nTerms should be reviewed in each sales jurisdiction.\n\n### Crypto, AML, and sanctions\n\nSelf-hosting a wallet removes a payment processor but not all regulation. EU Regulation 2023/1113 applies traceability requirements where a relevant payment service provider or crypto-asset service provider is involved. The regulation excludes certain person-to-person transfers conducted without a crypto-asset service provider, but transfers from or to regulated exchanges can bring CASP obligations into the transaction chain. citeturn18search0turn18search8\n\nSanctions rules may apply independently of whether customer identity verification is otherwise required. OFAC publishes sanctions lists and compliance guidance specifically addressing virtual-currency activity. citeturn18search1turn18search9 A privacy service should obtain jurisdiction-specific advice on whether it must screen names, wallet addresses, countries, or other indicators—and how to do so without collecting more personal data than the law requires.\n\nMarketing language must be precise. “We do not know your identity” is potentially misleading when the processor knows it and the merchant can access processor data. Better language is:\n\n> We do not require identity information for the VPN account. Card and bank intermediaries process payment information. We use separate random tokens so those intermediaries do not receive the VPN account or WireGuard key.\n\n## Recommended architecture and implementation roadmap\n\n### Target architecture\n\nThe recommended design is a **voucher-first entitlement system**:\n\n```text\nAnonymous account capability\n        |\n        v\nEntitlement ledger ----> Authentication service ----> WireGuard public-key service\n        ^\n        |\nAnonymous bearer voucher\n        ^\n        |\nPayment-specific issuance authorization\n        ^\n        |\nCard / bank / crypto / cash / reseller\n```\n\nThe preferred channels are:\n\n| Priority | Channel | Recommended implementation |\n|---:|---|---|\n| Highest privacy | Physical reseller voucher purchased with cash | Reseller receives inventory; VPN receives wholesale batch settlement; redemption is not reported to reseller |\n| High privacy | Self-hosted Monero or Bitcoin | Payment buys an anonymous voucher, not direct account time |\n| High separation with card convenience | Reseller or merchant-of-record voucher | Retail merchant handles card identity and chargeback; VPN receives no payer record |\n| Moderate separation | Direct card purchase of blind or ordinary voucher | Hosted checkout, random purchase handle, no customer profile, 3DS, separate redemption |\n| Operational fallback | Bank credit transfer | Random payment reference converted into a voucher |\n| Niche | Mailed cash | Opaque token, dual-control physical processing, no identifying information requested |\n\n### Implementation sequence\n\n1. **Define the threat model and claims.**  \n   Document whether the objective is protection from a processor, reseller, database breach, insider, subpoena, collusion, or global observer. Do not use “anonymous” without identifying which party lacks which information.\n\n2. **Create random account capabilities.**  \n   Generate at least 128 bits of entropy. Do not require an email, name, phone number, username, or password. Provide an optional locally stored recovery key or split recovery code.\n\n3. **Separate service principals from account capabilities.**  \n   The account database holds the capability commitment and expiry. The WireGuard control plane receives only an HMAC-derived service principal.\n\n4. **Require client-side WireGuard private-key generation.**  \n   The API accepts only public keys. Reject private-key fields and ensure crash reports and diagnostics redact account capabilities and keys. WireGuard’s official tooling supports local private-key generation and public-key derivation. citeturn20view10\n\n5. **Build the entitlement ledger before integrating payments.**  \n   Implement one operation: add a fixed amount of service time to an account. Its input must not include a processor transaction ID.\n\n6. **Implement voucher generation in a dedicated security domain.**  \n   Use a CSPRNG, at least 128 bits of entropy, a human-readable encoding, checksum, keyed database lookup, KMS/HSM-managed secrets, batch controls, and dual authorization for large issuance runs.\n\n7. **Implement atomic redemption.**  \n   Lock the voucher row, verify status, mark it spent, add entitlement, and commit in one transaction. Never persist `voucher_code -> account_id`.\n\n8. **Create append-only, privacy-preserving audit events.**  \n   Each domain gets its own event IDs. Events can contain:\n   - Payment settled or refunded.\n   - Voucher batch authorized.\n   - Voucher issued, redeemed, expired, or revoked.\n   - Anonymous entitlement duration added.\n   - WireGuard peer registered, rotated, or removed.\n\n   Do not use one global correlation ID.\n\n9. **Add aggregate reconciliation.**  \n   Reconcile daily totals instead of individual joins:\n\n   ```text\n   settled product units\n   = vouchers issued\n   + unconsumed issuance allowances\n   + approved refunds\n   + documented exceptions\n\n   redeemed voucher duration\n   = anonymous entitlement duration added\n   ```\n\n10. **Anchor audit integrity.**  \n    Hash-chain each domain’s events and produce a daily signed root. An auditor can verify that events were not altered without receiving the cross-domain data needed to reconstruct customer relationships.\n\n11. **Integrate self-hosted cryptocurrency.**  \n    Use separate addresses or invoices, sufficient confirmation policies, secure wallet separation, watch-only monitoring where possible, encrypted backups, and withdrawal keys kept outside the web tier. For Bitcoin, BTCPay is a strong baseline because it is self-hosted and validates through the merchant’s node. citeturn20view9\n\n12. **Integrate hosted card checkout.**  \n    Use Stripe Checkout, Elements, Braintree, or an equivalent PCI-compliant hosted component. Do not send account numbers in metadata. The metadata contains only a random purchase session. Stripe Elements can keep card data off the merchant server. citeturn21view9\n\n13. **Enable 3-D Secure and conservative limits.**  \n    Use low per-transaction limits, a small number of denomination choices, no anonymous high-value stored balances, and no bulk purchase without enhanced review. Treat 3-D Secure as loss reduction, not chargeback prevention. citeturn20view8\n\n14. **Issue a bearer entitlement after payment.**  \n    The initial version can return an ordinary random voucher. Delete the payment-to-voucher mapping after the legally and operationally required period. The advanced version should blind the voucher before signing.\n\n15. **Introduce a refund secret.**  \n    Return a second, non-redeemable secret tied only to the processor transaction. The user presents it to request a refund. Refund to the original payment instrument to reduce laundering and account-takeover risk.\n\n16. **Add independent reseller distribution.**  \n    Prefer fixed-value, scratch-covered cards. Contractually prohibit the reseller from receiving redemption information. Decide whether cards are preactivated or activated at sale:\n    - Pre-activated cards provide stronger sale/redemption separation but create inventory-theft risk.\n    - Per-code point-of-sale activation controls theft but lets the retailer correlate exact serial and sale.\n    - Batch activation is a useful compromise.\n\n17. **Design cash operations under dual control.**  \n    Separate envelope opening, counting, ledger entry, and banking. Use payment tokens, not account numbers. Establish rules for overpayment, underpayment, damaged currency, counterfeit notes, exchange rates, and abandoned payments.\n\n18. **Create retention classes and automated deletion.**  \n    Suggested baseline:\n    - Exact processor timestamp: dispute/refund period only.\n    - Processor transaction reference: encrypted and legally required duration or shortest practical support period.\n    - Payment-to-issuance authorization: until issuance plus short reconciliation window.\n    - Voucher-to-account relationship: never persisted.\n    - Voucher spent marker: until token expiry plus security margin.\n    - Old WireGuard public keys: deletion after rotation grace period.\n    - Accounting records: legal minimum, isolated from account systems.\n\n19. **Harden support workflows.**  \n    Support agents should never request card details, WireGuard private keys, full database exports, or VPN traffic information. Access to payment refunds and account adjustments should require different roles.\n\n20. **Commission three separate reviews.**  \n    Obtain a cryptographic review of token issuance, a payment/PCI and fraud review, and jurisdiction-specific legal review covering consumer rights, privacy, tax/accounting, sanctions, AML, stored value, money transmission, and VPN regulation.\n\n### Audit evidence that preserves privacy\n\nA useful independent audit should verify:\n\n| Control objective | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Payment systems have no VPN account IDs | Schemas, data-flow tracing, access-control tests |\n| WireGuard systems have no payment references | Schema and source-code review |\n| Private keys are client-generated | Application code and API validation |\n| Voucher redemption does not persist account linkage | Transaction tests and post-redemption database inspection |\n| Processor IDs are automatically deleted or isolated | Retention jobs, deletion logs, sampling |\n| Logs redact secrets | Log-pipeline tests with synthetic vouchers and account numbers |\n| Resellers receive no redemption data | API inventory and contract review |\n| Crypto is self-hosted | Node, wallet, network, and key-management inspection |\n| No hidden analytics correlate payment and redemption | DNS, frontend, mobile-app, and telemetry review |\n| Audit events are tamper-evident | Hash-chain verification and signed daily roots |\n\nThe public transparency report should publish system diagrams, retained fields, deletion intervals, payment-subprocessor names, voucher behavior, audit scope, and known limitations. It should not merely claim “no logs.”\n\n## Source hierarchy and legal caveats\n\nThe sources should be prioritized in this order:\n\n| Priority | Source class | Examples used in this analysis |\n|---:|---|---|\n| Highest | Provider policies describing actual retained fields and payment flows | Mullvad no-logging policy, privacy policy, terms, reseller page, and refund policy; IVPN privacy and payment documentation. citeturn20view0turn20view3turn21view1turn19view4 |\n| Highest | Statutes and consolidated official legal texts | GDPR, PSD2, EU Consumer Rights Directive, and EU crypto-transfer regulation. citeturn16view0turn17view0turn17view1turn17view3turn18search0 |\n| High | Official technical standards | WireGuard documentation and IETF Privacy Pass RFCs. citeturn20view10turn20view11 |\n| High | Processor documentation | Stripe tokenization, KYC, 3-D Secure, liability shift, and dispute documentation; Adyen onboarding documentation. citeturn20view8turn21view8turn21view9turn14search2 |\n| High | Financial-regulator guidance | CFPB billing-dispute rules, FinCEN virtual-currency guidance, EBA limited-network guidance, and OFAC sanctions resources. citeturn15search3turn18search2turn18search7turn18search1 |\n| Secondary | Marketing pages, reseller descriptions, forums, or reviews | Useful for discovery, but insufficient for claims about retained data, legal status, or actual technical separation |\n\nThe legal conclusions remain necessarily provisional because the jurisdiction is unspecified. The operator’s incorporation, banking country, customer locations, reseller countries, voucher transferability, cryptocurrency custody, transaction limits, refund terms, and corporate structure can change the analysis.\n\nThe central conclusions are nevertheless stable:\n\n- **Cards and banks can be decoupled from VPN accounts, but the financial transaction itself will not be anonymous to the regulated payment chain.**\n- **Chargebacks on cards can be reduced or shifted, not completely prevented.**\n- **Reseller vouchers are the best practical combination of card acceptance and VPN-side anonymity.**\n- **Blindly issued bearer entitlements provide the strongest technical unlinkability, at the expense of refunds, revocation, support, and implementation complexity.**\n- **WireGuard private keys should be client-generated and never enter payment systems.**\n- **A privacy-preserving service must accept some fraud loss rather than secretly rebuild identity linkage through telemetry, device fingerprinting, or long-term correlation records.**
OvsdbMirror (the list_dbs/get_bridge_state RPCs failing in the Explorer) is the old design: it proxies over D-Bus to a bus name org.opdbus.v1.mirror (crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/grpc_server.rs:2177) that nothing registers anymore — I checked busctl list, it's not running. That's the exact "D-Bus-passthrough mirror" pattern CLAUDE.md flags as deprecated (same family as the removed op-openvswitch-daemon).\n\nReplacement: the ovsdb_bridge plugin (crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/ovsdb_bridge.rs) — it wraps op_network::rovs_proxy (native OVSDB JSON-RPC, no D-Bus passthrough, no CLI subprocess) and is a genuine 1:1 RFC 7047 mirror. It's already sealed and live in the current blob catalog as per-method gRPC services, confirmed in the reseal output earlier:\n\n- ListDbs → operation.method.ovsdb_bridge.list_dbs\n- GetSchema → operation.method.ovsdb_bridge.get_schema\n- Transact → operation.method.ovsdb_bridge.transact\n- Monitor/MonitorCond → operation.method.ovsdb_bridge.monitor / .monitor_cond\n- Echo, Lock/Unlock/Steal, Cancel — all present too\n\nFor GetBridgeState specifically: there's no single aggregate call in the new design — that was OvsdbMirror parsing a raw dump into a Bridge→Port→Interface tree client-side. The decomposed equivalent is rovs_commands.list_bridges + rovs_commands.list_ports (crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/rovs_commands.rs), which hit the same native rovs client directly.\n\nSo in the gRPC Explorer: swap OvsdbMirror/ListDbs → ovsdb_bridge/list_dbs, and OvsdbMirror/GetBridgeState → rovs_commands/list_bridges (+ list_ports if you need port detail too).
